detail.abstract:
Global maritime supply chains are increasingly exposed to stochastic disruptions at port– hinterland interfaces, including terminal capacity shocks, congestion propagation, and demand volatility. Conventional recovery strategies typically rely on centralized rescheduling and largescale optimization, which often become computationally intractable or operationally slow in complex intermodal systems.&nbsp;
<div>
 This paper introduces a Capacity-Geometry Framework for analyzing the resilience of maritime intermodal networks under disruption dynamics. The framework models the network as a decentralized decision system in which operational constraints such as terminal capacity limits, queue restrictions, and intermodal transfer capacities, define a geometric feasible operating space. Disruptive events are represented as contractions of this feasible space, reflecting the sudden loss of operational capacity within the network.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Within this representation, localized operational decisions, including terminal queue management and intermodal flow routing, are modeled as dynamic state variables governed by nonlinear system dynamics. Using methods from variational inequality theory and dynamical systems, we establish sufficient conditions under which the disrupted network exhibits endogenous stabilization, meaning that the system naturally converges toward a new equilibrium without requiring centralized coordination or global rescheduling.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Computational experiments conducted on a large-scale synthetic intermodal hub demonstrate that the proposed decentralized framework significantly accelerates disruption recovery, suppresses queue propagation across terminal interfaces, and reduces computational overhead relative to conventional centralized optimization approaches.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The results provide a mathematically grounded perspective on maritime logistics resilience and introduce a new paradigm for designing self-stabilizing intermodal transport networks capable of adapting to disruption dynamics in real time.
</div>
